window.docContents[48].resolve({"/Cliques/#section-the-finset-of-n-cliques":{"contents":"If G is a finite graph with a Fintype V instance then we can form the Finset of\nall n-cliques in G.\n\nsection more_cliques\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n#source cliqueFinset -- def cliqueFinset (n : ℕ) : Finset (Finset α) :=\n                     --   {s | G.IsNClique n s}\n\n\nRecall our discussion of Set-Builder Notation. The finset\nG.cliqueFinset n is univ : Finset (Finset V), i.e. the powerset of V,\nfiltered for those Finsets that are n-cliques in G.\n\nexample [Fintype V] (n : ℕ) [DecidableRel G.Adj] [DecidableEq V] :\n  G.cliqueFinset n =\n     (Finset.univ : Finset (Finset V)).filter (G.IsNClique n · ) := rfl\n\n\nThis is computable so we can use #eval to compute the Finset (Finset V) of all\nn-cliques in a finite graph G : SimpleGraph V.\n\n#eval C[5].cliqueFinset 2     -- {{0, 1}, {1, 2}, {2, 3}, {0, 4}, {3, 4}}\n#eval mμ(K[4]).cliqueFinset 3 -- {{0ˡ, 1ˡ, 2ˡ}, {0ˡ, 1ˡ, 3ˡ},..., {1ˡ, 2ˡ, 3ʳ}}\n#eval mμ(C[5]).cliqueFinset 5 -- ∅\n\n\nThe cliqueFinset of n-cliques in a finite graph is empty if and only if the graph\nis n-clique-free.\n\n#check cliqueFinset_eq_empty_iff -- G.cliqueFinset n = ∅ ↔ G.CliqueFree n\n\n\nHence we can decide if a finite graph is n-clique-free by checking if\nG.cliqueFinset n = ∅.\n\ninstance decidableCliqueFree [Fintype V] (n : ℕ) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [DecidableEq V] : Decidable (G.CliqueFree n) :=\n  decidable_of_iff _ cliqueFinset_eq_empty_iff\n\n\n#eval C[5].CliqueFree 3     -- true\n#eval mμ(C[5]).CliqueFree 3 -- true\n#eval K[4].CliqueFree 3     -- false\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques","header":"6.4. The Finset of n-Cliques","id":"/Cliques/#section-the-finset-of-n-cliques"},"/Introduction/#section-finding-lemmas":{"contents":"Much of the work of formalizing is finding the right lemma in Mathlib. Mathlib's\nnaming conventions (see Naming Conventions) help, since a name\nshould describe the statement (for example card_neighborFinset_eq_degree), but they\nonly get you so far: the degree of a vertex in a complete graph is\ncomplete_graph_degree, not degree_top. So it is worth knowing how to search.\n\n* The tactic exact? searches Mathlib for a lemma that closes the current goal, and\napply? and rw? do the same for apply and rw.* Loogle searches by constants and expressions, for\nexample SimpleGraph.degree and SimpleGraph.completeGraph.* LeanSearch searches using natural language, for example\n\"degree of a vertex in the complete graph\".* Mathlib's documentation pages list the declarations of each file in order, so the\nlemmas about a definition can usually be found near it.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.3. Finding Lemmas","id":"/Introduction/#section-finding-lemmas"}});